steps.action:
Estimates the slip distribution and rupture history on Fault 1:
- three asperities, with maximum slip of about 3 m at 30–45 km depth;
- rake varying with depth;
- rupture velocity of about 3.0 km/s, propagating northwest;
- seismic moment of 7.56 × 10^19 N·m and duration of about 26 s;
- stress drop of 0.81 MPa.

Checks the solution in three ways:
- data fits (misfits of 0.227 teleseismic, 0.292 local, 0.144 GPS);
- aftershock locations against the slip areas;
- the inferred rupture velocity against the shear-wave velocity of the 3D velocity model (Huang et al. 2014).

Designs a four-case simulation ablation:
- (A) inverted source with the 3D model;
- (B) inverted source with a homogeneous half-space;
- (C) the same slip but rupturing eastward, in a half-space;
- (D) a double-couple point source in a half-space.

Compares the cases with each other:
- removing the basin (B) weakens Taipei PGA;
- reversing directivity (C) weakens northern PGA;
- the point source (D) puts the north in an enlarged radiation quadrant.

Proposes four future rupture scenarios for the southern Ryukyu zone, drawing on the author's earlier Solomon and Illapel inversions and on global megathrust events:
- mid-depth rupture;
- shallow tsunami earthquake;
- megathrust;
- deep-to-trench composite megathrust.
